00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.040 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  I'm Edward I'm the head teacher  mathematics at Tobruk Technology High  School where a school almost 2,000  students with an incredibly broad  cross-section of students we're 58%  language background other than English  and we have a whole range of students in  terms of their achievement level in  mathematics from people who are the  highest academic performers to students  who struggle at every step of the way in  order to grapple with mathematics and  become confident and competent in it i  integrate collaborative problem-solving  into every lesson that I teach because I  view it as really central to the way  that people learn mathematics and the  way that they do mathematics out in the  real world some of the things that are  important to having that as part of my  classroom is making sure that students  have time and opportunities to interact  with each other  to give them the kinds of problems that  actually reward to them to talk to  another student who actually try and  wrap their head around another  perspective on a problem or another  solution to a problem that's equally  valid but just not the one that they  thought of to begin with and also making  sure that I pose problems that are  challenging enough but also accessible  enough for all students to be able to  have some success with experience you  know the ability to start on the problem  but also for the problem to have natural  extensions that can take even the most  competent mathematicians off into new  territory  when I think about collaborative  problem-solving in the classroom one of  the things that's really important is to  recognize that it is an intimidating  kind of experience for a lot of students  who maybe aren't as confident and  they're afraid to put their foot forward  or put their hand up or shout something  out that all their peers are going to  hear and maybe judge so one of the most  critical roles for the teacher is to be  able to take all of those interactions  and to speak and question in such a way  as to show students mistakes and ideas  and risk-taking are all welcome in the  classroom and so much terms on the way  you look at a child the way your tone of  voice is moving your posture and how you  textin and skin put and whether it's  exactly the answer you are searching for  or not you help students understand yes  your contribution to this classroom to  this lesson to your peers your  contributions of value so I think that's  a really important piece to make sure  that every student across the spectrum  is engaged all the working  mathematically skills that are  integrated into the syllabus  understanding fluency communicating  reasoning and problem-solving really  have to import into every single lesson  it takes a lot of time to develop as a  teacher to number one understand what  does it look like to actually do more  than just develop students ability to  repeat a procedure on a different set of  numbers that takes a lot of time and  that's really tricky working out how do  you question students how do you divide  up your time within the lesson to  actually cultivate those skills it took  me years and many missteps and working  out okay well this didn't work out well  how am I going to try and teach this  same concept again be able to bring in  these skills and make sure that students  understand okay this is more than just  repeating the steps of an algorithm this  is a whole different way of thinking I  think one of the reasons why it's so  important to devote that time and to be  willing to make mistakes as a teacher to  get that point is because our students  are really going to have a completely  deficient view of what mathematics is  it's all if all they're getting across  is okay I can answer questions I can get  a right number at the end and therefore  that's what makes me a good  mathematician and that's such a narrow  of what true mathematics ears so I think  that even though it takes so much time I  really think that if we don't invest  that time then really our students are  not getting across the real sense of  what mathematics is how valuable it is  to them and that's why I love seeing my  students talking to each other because  then I see right you're doing more than  just developing your own foundations I  can see you communicating that skill to  someone else I can see you communicating  the way you approach that idea to  someone else and I can see that person  who's listening gaining a new  perspective on a problem which they  didn't have before all of those kinds of  skills really are best developed when  students can work together on  problem-solving developing these skills  as teachers as a team of teachers as a  faculty that values using skills and  want our students to develop them takes  a lot of time and a lot of effort but  there are a lot of strategies that can  be put in place that help us as teachers  to develop this over time I think of  things like being willing to be observed  by peers and by our supervisors so that  we can gain a different perspective on  the way that we teach in the classroom  there are so many things that we do that  are helpful or unhelpful track kids that  we're not even conscious of because  we've been doing these for years there  are some strengths that we need to  emphasize and which an outside observer  can help us notice and say that's really  great keep on going with that and there  are other things which we realize oh I  didn't think that this was having this  effect on that gym or I didn't realize  that this came across it was unclear  that having someone else in the room can  really be helpful similar to that team  teaching as much as physically possible  it's such a wonderful resource  especially when you're trying to again  make sure that you differentiate across  your whole range of students and you  want to make sure students are getting  not just your way of approached but  someone else's and that'll extend them  or enable them to encounter ideas that  and work with mathematical problems that  they weren't able to solve for I think  lastly I would say that thinking really  carefully about assessment and  constantly asking that question of what  are we assessing why do we put marks on  this why is this something that we want  to include in a task that  giving to students when we keep on  asking that question we're doing more  than asking can you factorize can you  long divide we're looking for do you  have 30 here is that sense of reasoning  understood can you justify this argument  if we incorporate that and make that the  conversation that we're always having a  round of sester then so many things  follow from that and it helps gain our  perspective on the importance of  distance

